## Acceptance Audit Findings

- **`focusedSeatId` is not reset when switching events, contradicting the story's own Dev Notes guarantee**
  Violates: Task 4 (Dev Notes bullet under Task 4, "Auch bei `error: () => this.loadError.set(true)` bleibt `focusedSeatId` unverändert `null`") and, by extension, the same state-isolation intent that Task 3 explicitly requires for `selectedSeatIds` ("ein Seitenwechsel zu einer anderen Veranstaltung darf keine Auswahl der vorherigen Veranstaltung übernehmen").
  Evidence: In `sitzplan-page.ts`, the `constructor()` effect resets `seatMap`, `loadError`, and `selectedSeatIds` at the top of every run, but never resets `focusedSeatId`:
  ```ts
  effect((onCleanup) => {
    this.seatMap.set(null);
    this.loadError.set(false);
    this.selectedSeatIds.set(new Set());
    // no this.focusedSeatId.set(null) here
    const subscription = this.seatMapService.getSeatMap(this.id()).subscribe({
      next: (seatMap) => {
        this.seatMap.set(seatMap);
        this.focusedSeatId.set(seatMap.seats.find((s) => s.status === 'Free')?.seatId ?? null);
      },
      error: () => this.loadError.set(true)
    });
    ...
  });
  ```
  If a user selects/focuses a seat in event A (which sets `focusedSeatId` via `onSeatToggled` or arrow-key navigation) and then switches to event B whose request errors, `focusedSeatId` keeps event A's stale seat id instead of becoming `null` as the Dev Notes assert. The Dev Notes text literally claims this is "bereits durch den Reset am effect-Anfang sichergestellt" (already ensured by the reset at the start of the effect), but no such reset line exists in the diff — the claim is unimplemented. Real-world UI impact is limited (the grid isn't rendered while `loadError` is true, so `onGridKeydown`'s `!map` guard prevents a crash), but it is a genuine, demonstrable gap between the prescribed/claimed behavior and the shipped code, and it is not covered by any of the 7 new `sitzplan-page.spec.ts` tests (the one id-change test only asserts `.seat--selected` count, not `focusedSeatId`/error-path behavior).

- **Dev Agent Record's self-verification claim is inaccurate**
  Violates: internal consistency of the story file (Dev Agent Record → Completion Notes List), not an AC per se, but relevant to review trust.
  Evidence: The story's Completion Notes claim "Tasks 1–4 ... gegen Story-Text/Diff verifiziert, keine Abweichungen gefunden" (verified against story text/diff, no deviations found). This is contradicted by the finding above — the Task 4 error-path guarantee was not actually implemented, so the self-review's "no deviations" claim does not hold up.

No other deviations found: AC1 (terracotta fill, white check, `border: none`), AC2 (toggle-off on second click), AC3 (occupied guard in `onActivate`, `tabindex` fully removed via `[attr.tabindex]="... ? null : ..."`, no hover/cursor on occupied), and the rest of AC4 (multi-select via `Set`, roving-tabindex arrow navigation with aisle/occupied skipping via `status === 'Free'` filtering, three-state `ariaLabel`, initial focus on first free seat) all match the prescribed snippets in `seat.ts`/`seat.html`/`seat.scss`/`sitzplan-page.ts`/`sitzplan-page.html` essentially verbatim. The documented scope exclusions (no price/category in `ariaLabel`, no summary panel, no booking POST) are correctly respected — no premature work from Stories 2.3–2.5 was pulled in. The `focusSeat()` type-cast change (`querySelector<HTMLElement>` → `(querySelector(...) as HTMLElement | null)`) is a documented, behavior-neutral TypeScript fix, not a spec deviation.

